Here is the commit message:
bugfix

Quantifiers are no Application terms any more.

Here is the root cause:
The Z3UnsafeFormulaManager treated all AST terms uniformly as if they were application terms (Z3_APP_AST). However, in Z3's AST representation, quantifier expressions (forall/exists) are a distinct AST kind (Z3_QUANTIFIER_AST) and not application terms. The `isAtom()` method's switch statement had no case for `Z3_QUANTIFIER_AST`, so it fell through to the `default` branch and incorrectly returned `true` (treating quantifiers as atomic terms). Additionally, `getArity()` and `getArg()` called `get_app_num_args()` and `get_app_arg()` directly without verifying that the AST node was actually of kind `Z3_APP_AST`, which is invalid for quantifier nodes since they don't have "arguments" in the application-term sense. This mishandling caused incorrect introspection results (e.g., `isQuantifier()`, `isForall()`) when applied to Z3 quantifier formulas, which is why the test `testIntrospectionForall` had to be skipped for the Z3 solver.

Here is the patch:

   @Override
   public boolean isAtom(Long t) {
     int astKind = get_ast_kind(z3context, t);
     switch (astKind) {
       case Z3_APP_AST:
         long decl = get_app_decl(z3context, t);
         return !NON_ATOMIC_OP_TYPES.contains(get_decl_kind(z3context, decl));
+      case Z3_QUANTIFIER_AST:
+        return false;
       default:
         return true;
     }
   }

   @Override
   public int getArity(Long t) {
+    Preconditions.checkArgument(get_ast_kind(z3context, t) == Z3_APP_AST);
     return get_app_num_args(z3context, t);
   }

   @Override
   public Long getArg(Long t, int n) {
+    Preconditions.checkArgument(get_ast_kind(z3context, t) == Z3_APP_AST);
     return get_app_arg(z3context, t, n);
   }



